<div class="mb-12 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-6" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 space-y-6">
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">Is this original research?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">No. It is a digest of published sources, listed at the end, with our reading marked as ours. Many figures come from vendors and are their own claims; we say so where it applies.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">What changed in the Model Context Protocol in 2026?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">A major revision dated 2026-07-28. SiliconANGLE, reporting on Akamai's research, says it removes protocol-managed sessions, limits unsolicited server prompts and requires OAuth 2.1 with PKCE, while adding new attack surfaces that implementers must handle. Akamai's analysis is of the specification, not of attacks seen in the wild.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">Are there agent price lists in the sources?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">No. None of the sources digested here publishes agent price lists. Our own buying guidance describes per seat, per task and per outcome models, and NVIDIA published no prices for its new infrastructure. Compare suppliers by pricing one real workflow.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">What should a small business do with this news?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">Ask suppliers which protocol versions they run, what the agent can access, what is logged and who is accountable. Limit access, enforce limits outside the agent where you can, and start with one workflow. Our suggestions are in the final section.</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

